# The .dag Format — Formal Specification v1.1

The `.dag` file format is a compiled, machine-optimized representation of a spec genome. It is the output of the spec engine — a Directed Acyclic Graph encoded as a JSON document. No prose. No markdown. Pure structure. Designed for AI agents to traverse with zero token waste.

## Grammar (JSON Schema)

```
dag-file ::= {
  "version": "1.1",
  "project": string,
  "compiled_at": ISO8601,
  "files": number,
  "nodes": [node, ...],
  "edges": [edge, ...],
  "predictions": [prediction, ...],
  "vectors": [vector, ...]
}

node ::= {
  "id": string,
  "file": string,          // source .dog file
  "chars": number,         // content length (for cost estimation)
  "properties": { string: { "type": string, "required": boolean, "constraints"?: string }, ... },
  "states": [string, ...],
  "lifecycle": [string, ...],
  "description": string    // for embedding
}

edge ::= {
  "source": string,
  "target": string,
  "verb": string,
  "cardinality": "1:1" | "1:N" | "N:1" | "N:M",
  "required": boolean,
  "cascade": "none" | "delete" | "nullify" | "restrict",
  "file": string,
  "section": string
}

prediction ::= {
  "id": string,
  "statement": string,
  "trigger": string | null,
  "timeframe": string | null,
  "confidence": 0.0-1.0,
  "measurement": string,
  "actual": "pending" | "correct" | "wrong" | "partially_correct"
}

vector ::= {
  "node_id": string,
  "content": string,
  "embedding": [number, ...],
  "model": "all-MiniLM-L6-v2" | "text-embedding-3-small"
}
```

## Validation Rules

### MUST (critical — graph is invalid)

| Rule | Check |
|------|-------|
| R1 | Valid JSON, parseable without error |
| R2 | `version` field present and begins with "1." |
| R3 | `project` field is non-empty string |
| R4 | Every edge `source` references an existing node `id` |
| R5 | Every edge `target` references an existing node `id` |
| R6 | Graph is acyclic — no circular dependencies |

### SHOULD (warning — graph is incomplete)

| Rule | Check |
|------|-------|
| R7 | Every node has a non-empty `description` |
| R8 | Every node has at least one property defined |
| R9 | Every node has `states` and `lifecycle` |
| R10 | At least one edge connects nodes |

## Edge Cases

| Case | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| Empty graph (no nodes) | Valid. Project has no entities defined. |
| Orphan node (no edges) | Valid. Node has no relationships yet. |
| Missing optional fields | Valid. `predictions`, `vectors` can be empty arrays. |
| Very large graph (1000+ nodes) | Consumer should paginate. No enforced limit. |
| Duplicate node IDs | Last definition wins. Consumer warns. |

## Token Efficiency

The `.dag` format is optimized for AI agents. Compared to reading `.dog` files:

```
.dog (read 10 files):    ~8000 tokens (prose, markdown, YAML)
.dag (read graph):       ~1200 tokens (JSON, no prose)
                         ──────
                         85% token savings per query
```

The compile step pays the parsing cost once. Every agent query saves.

## MCP API

The `spec serve` MCP server exposes the graph to AI agents over stdio:

| Tool | Input | Output |
|------|-------|--------|
| listProjects | — | Array of project names |
| listNodes | project | Array of nodes |
| getEntity | project, name | Single node with properties, states |
| getEdges | project, node | All edges to/from a node |
| getGraph | project | Full .dag JSON |

Agent workflow:
```
1. listProjects → ["spec-platform"]
2. getEntity("spec-platform", "Payment") → {id, properties, states, lifecycle}
3. getEdges("spec-platform", "Payment") → [{source, target, verb, cardinality}]
4. Agent has exact types, states, and connections. Zero hallucination.
```

## Pipeline

```
.dog files → spec parse → AST → spec compile → .dag → spec serve (MCP) → AI agent
```

## Conformance

An implementation is conforming if it:
1. Produces valid JSON matching the grammar
2. Validates all MUST rules
3. Handles all documented edge cases
4. Exposes the MCP API verbatim or equivalent

